AB  - Cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection is a major cause of morbidity in patients undergoing solid organ transplantation. CMV is widely distributed in the general population with seroprevalence rates ranging from 30 to 97% (1). After primary infection, CMV establishes life-long latency with multiple cell types having been shown to harbor the CMV genome. CMV infection primarily occurs in the first 3 months following transplant, but can be delayed in patients receiving CMV prophylaxis CMV infection can be defined as evidence of CMV replication regardless of symptoms. CMV disease generally refers to evidence of CMV infection with attributable symptoms. CMV disease can be further categorized as a‘viral syndrome’ with fever and malaise, ±leukopenia, thrombocytopenia or as tissue invasive disease (e.g. pneumonitis, hepatitis, retinitis, gastrointestinal disease). CMV has a predilection to invade the allograft likely in part due to an aberrant immune response within the allograft (1). In the absence of antiviral prophylaxis, the risk of symptomatic disease occurring is highest in heart-lung recipients (39%) and lowest in kidney transplant recipients (8%) (1).
