TY  - JOUR
TI  - Case 3: new clinical trials in renal transplantation
AU  - Sundaram Hariharan
JO  - Transplantation
PY  - 2003
VL  - 75
IS  - 7
SP  - 1084
EP  - 1087
DO  - 10.1097/01.tp.0000062661.17678.e4
AN  - 12698112
AB  - CLINICAL CASE In 1980, JT received a cadaveric kidney transplant for end-stage renal disease due to membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis when he was 15 years of age. He was maintained on an immunosuppressive regimen of azathioprine and prednisone. In 1997, his serum creatinine (SCr) level rose to 2.0 mg/dL, and chronic rejection was diagnosed by transplant renal biopsy. Despite excellent blood pressure control with an ACE inhibitor, his renal dysfunction progressed through May 2001, when his creatinine clearance was calculated to be 20 ml/min. Evaluation for retransplantation revealed a panel reactive antibody (PRA) result of 25%. JT’s brother proved to be a one-haplotype HLA match, and living donor transplantation was performed on December 15, 2001. At that time, JT remained off dialysis with creatinine clearance at 12 ml/min. At the time of repeat transplantation, JT had the following questions: 1. What is the best immunosuppressive regimen for me following this transplantation? 2. How will my medical management following this transplant differ from the management I received in 1980? DISCUSSION This clinical case allows us to evaluate clinically meaningful past and present endpoints, and potential future endpoints, for clinical trials in renal transplantation. The case also allows us to provide answers to concerns typical in patients undergoing repeat renal transplantation. Role of Clinical Trials in Transplantation Advances in immunosuppressive treatment are based on the results of evidence-based clinical trials. In the late 1970s and early 1980s, graft and patient survival at 1 year following transplantation were suboptimal. Therefore, it was reasonable to design clinical trials of immunosuppressive agents focusing on these short-term outcomes. Dramatic improvement in patient and graft survival followed the introduction of cyclosporine (CsA) in 1983 (1,2). A review of the results of clinical trials of immunosuppressive agents introduced after this time provides the opportunity to address JT’s questions to the transplant team. 1. What Is the Best Immunosuppressive Regimen for Me Following This Transplantation? JT’s initial immunosuppressive management reflects the standard of therapy in transplantation in 1980. The selection of immunosuppressive agents for management following JT’s second kidney allograft will be based on evidence from numerous clinical trials in renal transplantation completed over the past 20 years. Since JT’s primary transplant, clinical trials have confirmed the value of the calcineurin inhibitor cyclosporine (CsA) in kidney transplantation (1,2). A second calcineurin inhibitor, tacrolimus, introduced in 1994, was shown to have additional short- and long-term benefits. Compared to CsA, tacrolimus has been shown to reduce the incidence of acute rejection over the first year of follow-up (3,4). While intent-to-treat analysis revealed no difference in graft and patient survival, when crossover to tacrolimus or CsA for acute rejection was counted as treatment failure, there was a graft survival benefit at 5 years for patients on tacrolimus therapy (63.8% vs. 53.8%;P =0.014) (5). Since its approval for prevention of acute rejection in kidney transplantation in 1995, MMF has replaced azathioprine in the majority of transplant centers across the US. The combination of MMF with CsA (6), and more recently with tacrolimus (7–9), has reduced short-term acute rejection rates. In fact, MMF in combination with tacrolimus appears to be more effective in preventing acute rejection than when combined with CsA-Neoral (7). Long-term survival benefits with MMF have been shown in large database analyses (10,11). Transplant recipients at increased risk for acute rejection and poor graft outcome include African American patients, those with delayed graft function or high PRA, and those undergoing repeat transplantation (12). The induction agents available for clinical use, mostly in these high-risk candidates, are monoclonal OKT3 (introduced for the treatment of ongoing acute rejection), the polyclonal reagents ATGAM and Thymoglobulin, and the monoclonal anti-IL-2-receptor-α antibodies daclizumab and basiliximab (13–16). In the present clinical case, JT is at relatively high risk for acute rejection due to his elevated PRA and repeat transplant status and thus may benefit from receiving induction therapy. Thymoglobulin has been compared to basiliximab for high-risk cadaveric kidney recipients (17). The results reveal better outcome at 6 months in the Thymoglobulin treatment group, as evaluated by a composite endpoint (94% vs. 63%;P =0.0005). Although the benefit of Thymoglobulin has not been shown in living donor recipients, it is reasonable to consider Thymoglobulin therapy for this recipient. Alternatively, the use of an anti-IL-2-receptor-α antibody might be justified since JT is the recipient of a living donor organ characterized by spontaneous graft function. Information from clinical trials and database analyses clearly indicates that acute rejection is a known risk factor for poor long-term outcome (18,19). Therefore, the choice of maintenance immunosuppressive therapy for a patient with JT’s clinical profile is tacrolimus combined with MMF. Induction treatment with an IL-2-receptor blocker is also a reasonable choice, considering his pretransplant risk level. 2. How Will My Medical Management Following This Transplant Differ from The Management I Received in 1980? In 2002, the focus of JT’s care is to limit the risk of chronic rejection by preventing acute rejection and minimize cardiovascular risk in order to optimize long-term survival. Improvements in therapy since 1980 include the availability of MMF, tacrolimus, and induction immunosuppressive agents. Following this second transplant, it is likely that JT will benefit from a schedule of protocol biopsies which may identify any episodes of subclinical acute rejection (20). In turn, prompt treatment of early or mild rejection might prevent irreversible damage and improve long-term function of the allograft (21). Cardiovascular disease claims approximately 50% of all transplant patients who die with a functioning graft after the first year following transplantation (22,23). Therefore, monitoring JT closely for cardiovascular risk factors including hypertension, hyperlipidemia, and diabetes will be an important part of his follow-up (23–25). Infection accounts for as much as 26% of all renal allograft loss after the first year following transplantation (22). A regular schedule of monitoring for infectious diseases including cytomegalovirus (CMV) will be an important part of JT’s care. Ganciclovir will be administered for prophylaxis of cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection over the first 3 months following transplantation (26). Trimelnoprim, sulfamethoxazole or pentamidine will be administered as prophylactic therapy for Pneumocystis carinii pneumonia (PCP) (27). Increasing prevalence of polyomavirus infection is creating a need for surveillance using urine cytology and blood PCR estimation (28–31). Because chronically immunosuppressed patients may develop malignancies (23), JT will be monitored at regular intervals for the development of posttransplant lymphoproliferative disorder (PTLD) and skin cancers. Other potential complications associated with immunosuppression include hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidemia, and acute gout. Therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM) of tacrolimus will minimize adverse events and optimize effectiveness (23). Bone densitometry and assessment of thyroid function and hormone levels will be performed to assess JT’s bone and mineral metabolism. Early introduction of bisphosphonate therapy (e.g., alendronate) will be considered to prevent osteoporosis and osteopenia (32–34). New Clinical Trials in Transplantation Clinical trial experience and transplant registry analyses have revealed that outcomes among transplant patients correlate with a spectrum of identifiable risk factors (12). Modifying immunosuppressive regimens to target the heterogeneous risk profiles of transplant patients must be based on clinical trial results. In addition, as short-term results improve, the traditional endpoints of clinical trials are less useful in guiding clinical practice. Therefore, it will be essential to measure long-term endpoints or, preferably, to identify short-term markers that can predict long-term survival. Minimization of Immunosuppression in Low-risk Recipients Future clinical trials in transplantation will be designed to minimize immunosuppression-related side effects and to optimize long-term outcomes. Long-term morbidity associated with hypertension, glucose dysregulation, bone loss, and hyperlipidemia has made corticosteroid elimination appealing. Because unfavorable results have been reported with long-term follow-up (3–5 years) of corticosteroid minimization trials (35,36), interest in steroid avoidance trials has grown. Similarly, minimization of calcineurin inhibitors is under consideration as a strategy for improving long-term outcomes. Well-designed, controlled clinical trials are necessary to determine the risks and benefits of these simplified immunosuppressive regimens. Currently, the target population for these strategies is low-risk patients. Because even a single episode of acute rejection has been correlated with an approximate 50% reduction in renal allograft half-life (45±11 years vs. 25±8 years), trials that minimize the use of immunosuppressive agents must be conducted with great care (37). Optimization of Immunosuppression in High-risk Patients We are unable to test newer agents or combination therapy in registration-quality trials. However, it is a priority to evaluate the safety and efficacy of treatments in recipients at high risk for poor graft outcome. As described previously, transplant registry analyses have identified patients at high risk for poor graft outcome (12). While high-risk patients represent subsets of recipients enrolled into large-scale clinical trials, they rarely constitute the principal population of patients studied. Clinical study devoted to improving outcomes in high-risk patients is a priority in transplantation. Trials Using Alternative Endpoints At the time of the clinical trials of MMF in transplantation, graft survival at 1 year approached or exceeded 90% in most experienced transplant centers. Patient survival was very close to 100%. This progress suggests that measurement of endpoints from historical clinical trials, such as death, graft loss, and acute rejection cannot produce clinically meaningful results. The need to identify short-term endpoints with predictive value for long-term outcomes has led to the investigation of various clinical measurements as endpoints in future transplant clinical trials. Among the candidate endpoints are renal function at 6 months or 1 year and protocol (surveillance) renal biopsy results over the first 3 months following transplantation (20,21,38). The value of these short-term endpoints may be measured by their ability to predict long-term outcomes. Recent evidence suggests that serum creatinine levels ≤1.5 mg/dL and no change in serum creatinine of >0.3 mg/dL over the first year following transplantation may be indicative of good long-term graft survival (38). Maintaining excellent renal function was especially important in patients who experienced acute rejection episodes. Over the past decade, surveillance (protocol) biopsy of renal allografts has revealed a surprisingly higher-than-predicted incidence of subclinical acute rejection over the first 3 months following transplantation (20). Further investigation has revealed the possible long-term benefits of treating these subclinical bouts of rejection (21). Findings to date suggest that the incidence of subclinical acute rejection as determined by surveillance biopsy should be considered as an endpoint of clinical trials. Both the risks and discomfort associated with protocol biopsy should be considered in evaluating the ultimate benefit of this strategy in planning future clinical trials and practice. The Chronic Allograft Damage Index (CADI) scores renal allograft damage based on the status of 6 histological markers, namely, interstitial lymphocytic inflammation and fibrosis, glomerular mesangial matrix increase and sclerosis, vascular intimal proliferation, and tubular atrophy (39). In one recent study, the 1-year CADI score was found to be significantly associated with graft loss or patient death at 3 years posttransplant (P =0.0014) (40). The predictive value of CADI for long-term allograft function and outcome has not yet been validated. Endpoints that reflect morbidity and mortality should also be addressed in future clinical trials. With the improvements in patient and graft survival, effective prevention and management of comorbid conditions like cardiovascular disease, osteoporosis and diabetes become increasingly important. CONCLUSION The present clinical case has provided us with the opportunity to compare the immunosuppressive management of renal allograft recipients transplanted over the past 2 decades. While immunosuppressive agents have changed, the goals of medical management remain the same: to ensure excellent long-term graft function and optimal health of the renal allograft recipient. Future clinical trials in transplantation will be designed to meet these goals. Among the transplant clinical trials urgently needed are those which will provide guidelines for the management of patients at specific levels of risk for poor graft outcome. Trials that test short-term surrogate markers of long-term outcomes are also a priority. Given the relatively small number of patients transplanted each year, strategies for optimizing clinical trial design are critical to the usefulness of the results of these trials.
