AN  - 21441850
AB  - The authors of the new cytomegalovirus (CMV) consensus guidelines (1) gave grade III recommendation for the use of valganciclovir in liver transplantation and stated that “in a subgroup analysis, valganciclovir was associated with a higher rate of tissue-invasive disease in liver transplant recipients (83), but its use is still recommended based on expert opinion; in one survey, it was the most commonly used drug for CMV prevention in liver transplant recipients.” First, the aforementioned statement implies that if people are using valganciclovir for liver transplant, then safety is not a major issue. This assumption is precarious because even in a center with a high annual number of liver transplantations, it would be extremely unlikely for the transplant team to be able to differentiate expected CMV cases from valganciclovir-associated cases of CMV disease. No questionnaire survey or expert opinion should invalidate the PV16000 results (2), which remain the strongest contraindication for the use of valganciclovir in liver recipients. Second, Ref. 83 of guidelines is the PV16000 trial (2), which, in fact, did not report any subgroup analysis for liver recipients, even though the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) analysis of the PV16000 liver recipients was already public in September 2003 (PV16000 was published in April 2004). The correct guideline reference is given in our letter's Ref. 3; this FDA safety alert is still available. Of note, up to today, no peer-reviewed scientific publication from Roche or PV16000 authors that could further clarify the FDA analysis has been published on the safety issues (e.g., CMV disease, CMV tissue-invasive disease, rejection, graft loss, leucopenia, morbidity, and mortality) related to the use of valganciclovir in liver recipients. Third, a comprehensive reanalysis of the PV16000 trial was recently published by our group (4). We showed that not only the rate of CMV tissue-invasive disease in liver recipients was 4.5 times significantly higher with valganciclovir compared with that with ganciclovir but also there was highly statistically significant interaction between liver transplant and valganciclovir for both CMV disease (P=0.008) and CMV tissue-invasive disease (P=0.009); these findings suggest that valganciclovir may be harmful to liver recipients and give further support to the FDA decision: “Valcyte is not indicated for use in liver transplant patients” (5). Based on the quality of evidence metrics used in the guidelines (1), valganciclovir should not be recommended for liver transplants with grade I evidence. Another issue with these guidelines is related to acyclovir. The guideline (1) cited Ref. 82 by Rubin et al. (6): “In early studies, acyclovir was determined to be inferior to ganciclovir for prevention of CMV.” However, this study (6) used suboptimal acyclovir doses (1.2 g/day), so it should obviously not be the supporting evidence against acyclovir. There are other trials that directly compared acyclovir against ganciclovir: two studies had substantial baseline imbalances for CMV disease risks (7, 8); three studies used acyclovir in both arms (8–10); three studies gave intravenous posttransplant therapy only to the ganciclovir arm (9, 11, 12); two studies used low-dose acyclovir (6, 8); and none of these studies were double-blinded. These head-to-head studies are not comparable for a pooled analysis. Therefore, the evidence for which drug (if any) is superior remains unanswered. We were surprised by the fact that high-dose acyclovir (3.2 g/day) was not recommended as a CMV prophylaxis regimen for any allograft recipient. This is in clear disagreement with the best evidence-based medicine. Two recent meta-analyses showed highly statistically significant benefits with acyclovir when compared with controls for universal prophylaxis: Kalil et al. (13)— randomized/high-dose acyclovir trials: 75% reduction in CMV disease (95% confidence interval 53%–86%; P<0.0001; I2=0%); and Hodson et al. (14)— randomized/all doses: 55% (95% confidence interval 31%–71%; P=0.0002; I2=7.8%). Importantly, these acyclovir effects remained beneficial after adjustments for study quality, allograft type, and CMV serologic mismatch. Further, two other meta-analyses that used different methodology and were independently performed by different research groups found similar statistically significant benefits with acyclovir (15, 16). The very low heterogeneity and sensitivity analysis of these meta-analyses corroborated the conclusion that “the critical appraisal of the best available randomized, controlled trials provides strong support that acyclovir and ganciclovir show significant beneficial effects in reducing CMV organ disease in the transplant population at high risk” (13). Hence, it is unequivocal that based on the quality of evidence metrics used by the guidelines (1), high-dose acyclovir should receive grade I evidence for CMV prophylaxis in kidney and liver recipients at high risk for CMV. In conclusion, it is concerning that this international CMV consensus guidelines dismissed the strongest evidence (grade I) against the use of valganciclovir in liver recipients and overlooked all the evidence (grade I) in favor of high-dose acyclovir for CMV prophylaxis. Andre C. Kalil Diana F. Florescu Infectious Diseases Division University of Nebraska Medical Center Omaha, NE
