AB  - OBJECTIVES: Late cytomegalovirus disease after completion of prophylactic therapy occurs in 5-21% of renal allograft recipients within the first year post-transplantation. Identifying patients at risk for late infection is clinically difficult; prolonged cytomegalovirus (CMV) monitoring is costly and cumbersome as follow-up intervals lengthen. PATIENTS AND METHODS: We performed a prospective 1 year study in 54 de novo renal recipients to assess the minimum CMV monitoring frequency for identifying patients at risk. RESULTS AND CONCLUSIONS: CMV DNA PCR monitoring on the last day, and again 2 weeks after conclusion of oral ganciclovir prophylaxis, seemed sufficient for identifying recipients at risk for developing clinically relevant late CMV disease and for whom closer clinical follow-up is warranted.
